# Game content and data

## NPC and scare design

- Give NPCs readable motivations, routines, high fears, medium fears, ineffective scares, a resistance, and eventually a personal clue.
- An ineffective scare must still teach the player something.
- Repetition should reduce novelty and encourage varied combinations.
- Possession must remain goofy, temporary, non-violent, and useful for cooperative scares.
- Successfully haunted NPCs may become harmless Haunted Helpers rather than disappearing or being harmed.

## Progression

- Match upgrades may affect power and tactical options.
- Permanent progression should mainly unlock cosmetics, variety, stories, and alternate effects rather than pay-to-win strength.
- Keep scoring transparent and reward discovery, variety, teamwork, and creativity.

## Online and child safety

When online features are eventually specified:

- Prefer private room codes and preset communication.
- Minimise personal data collection.
- Do not expose public profiles, precise location, or unrestricted user-generated text by default.
- Server authority must own NPC state, scores, and scare outcomes.
- Include mute, block, report, parental controls, and safe defaults before public matchmaking.

## Monetisation

Do not introduce loot boxes, gambling-like mechanics, manipulative countdowns, pay-to-win abilities, or pressure-based purchases. Any future purchase design must be transparent and appropriate for a family audience.
